
  Each gets its own spec → plan → build cycle. I'd brainstorm #1 with you now and carry the notification requirements
  forward as constraints, so the task model is shaped to support them rather than retrofitted.

  Before that, one fork decides whether this stays a static page or becomes a client/server system — and it's expensive
  to change later.

  [1mThe fork:[0m whether a notification can reach someone who doesn't have the page open.

  - [1mIn-app only[0m (badge, inbox, toast while the page is open). Can live entirely in the browser with local storage.
    Cheapest by a wide margin, no server, no accounts, no deliverability. Rules out reaching anyone who's away — which
    is most of when people want to be notified.
  - [1mEmail / push / SMS[0m — needs a server, persisted users, real addresses, a sending provider, bounce and unsubscribe
    handling. This is the bulk of the work in any real notifications system, and it forces #2 (users) to be real rather
    than a name in a dropdown.
  - [1mBoth, in-app first[0m — build the in-app inbox as the source of truth, add out-of-band delivery as a later channel over
    the same event log. Costs one abstraction (a channel interface) up front and keeps the door open.

  My recommendation is [1mboth, in-app first[0m, unless you already know this is single-user — the abstraction is cheap now
  and re-plumbing event delivery later is not. But if "users" here really means one person tracking their own tasks, say
  so and this whole thing shrinks to an activity log.
